      "text": "Since $\\sin E = 1$, we know that $E$ must be a right angle, so $\\angle E = 90^\\circ$.\nIn a right triangle, the sides opposite the angles form a right triangle, so we have $\\cos D = \\frac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$.\nSince $\\sin D = \\frac{5}{13}$, we can write $\\frac{8}{13} = \\frac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$, where $D$ is opposite the side with length $8$.\nWe have $\\text{adjacent}^2 + \\text{hypotenuse}^2 = (\\text{hypotenuse})^2$, so $\\text{adjacent}^2 = (\\text{hypotenuse})^2 - \\text{hypotenuse}^2 = -3^2 - 3^2 = -36$.  Thus, the hypotenuse is $\\sqrt{(-36)} = 3\\sqrt{2}$.\nTherefore, $\\sin F = \\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} = \\boxed{\\frac{8}{13}}$.The answer is: \\frac{8}{13}",
