      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Given in right triangle \\( DEF \\), we know:\n- \\( \\sin D = \\frac{5}{13} \\)\n- \\( \\sin E = 1 \\)\n\nFirst, recall the properties of a right triangle:\n- The sum of the angles in a triangle is \\(180^\\circ\\). Since \\( DEF \\) is a right triangle, \\( D + E + F = 180^\\circ \\). This implies \\( F = 180^\\circ - (D + E) \\).\n\nIn a right triangle, the sine function involves the sides opposite and the hypotenuse. Specifically, for angle \\( E \\):\n\\[\n\\sin E = \\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} = 1\n\\]\nThis implies that the opposite side to \\( E \\) is the hypotenuse itself. Therefore, \\( E = 90^\\circ \\).\n\nSince \\( F \\) is the remaining angle in the right triangle, we have:\n\\[\nF = 180^\\circ - (D + E) = 180^\\circ - 180^\\circ = 0^\\circ\n\\]\n\nHowever, this calculation seems to be incorrect due to a misunderstanding in the problem interpretation. Let's re-evaluate the given conditions carefully.\n\nGiven:\n\\[\n\\sin D = \\frac{5}{13}\n\\]\nThis implies:\n\\[\n\\sin D = \\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} = \\frac{5}{13}\n\\]\n\nSince \\( D \\) is an angle in a right triangle, the opposite side to \\( D \\) is 5 units and the hypotenuse is 13 units. We can use the Pythagorean theorem to find the adjacent side:\n\\[\n\\text{adjacent}^2 + 5^2 = 13^2\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{adjacent}^2 + 25 = 169\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{adjacent}^2 = 144\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{adjacent} = 12\n\\]\n\nThus, the sides of the triangle are 5, 12, and 13. The angle \\( D \\) is opposite the side 5, and we need to find \\( \\sin F \\).\n\nSince \\( F = 90^\\circ - D \\) (as \\( F \\) is the complement of \\( D \\) in a right triangle):\n\\[\n\\sin F = \\sin (90^\\circ - D) = \\cos D\n\\]\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity:\n\\[\n\\cos^2 D + \\sin^2 D = 1\n\\]\n\\[\n\\cos^2 D + \\left(\\frac{5}{13}\\right)^2 = 1\n\\]\n\\[\n\\cos^2 D + \\frac{25}{169} = 1\n\\]\n\\[\n\\cos^2 D = 1 - \\frac{25}{169}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\cos^2 D = \\frac{169}{169} - \\frac{25}{169}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\cos^2 D = \\frac{144}{169}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\cos D = \\sqrt{\\frac{144}{169}} = \\frac{12}{13}\n\\]\n\nThus, \\( \\sin F = \\cos D = \\frac{12}{13} \\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{12}{13}}\n\\]",
