      "text": "We are given that in right triangle \\( DEF \\), \\(\\sin D = \\frac{5}{13}\\) and \\(\\sin E = 1\\). We need to find \\(\\sin F\\).\n\nFirst, recall that in a right triangle, the sum of the non-right angles is \\(90^\\circ\\), so \\(F = 90^\\circ - D\\). The sine of \\(F\\) is the cosine of \\(D\\), and the sine of \\(D\\) is already given as \\(\\sin D = \\frac{5}{13}\\). Therefore, \\(\\cos D = \\sin F\\).\n\nNext, we need to determine \\(\\cos D\\). Since \\(\\sin^2 D + \\cos^2 D = 1\\), we can substitute \\(\\sin D = \\frac{5}{13}\\) into this identity:\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{5}{13}\\right)^2 + \\cos^2 D = 1\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{25}{169} + \\cos^2 D = 1\n\\]\n\\[\n\\cos^2 D = 1 - \\frac{25}{169}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\cos^2 D = \\frac{169}{169} - \\frac{25}{169}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\cos^2 D = \\frac{144}{169}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\cos D = \\sqrt{\\frac{144}{169}} = \\frac{12}{13}\n\\]\nSince \\(F = 90^\\circ - D\\), we have \\(\\sin F = \\cos D = \\frac{12}{13}\\).\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(\\sin F\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{12}{13}}\\).",
